# Specification & Gate Discipline

## Hierarchy

Implementation MUST NOT redefine normative semantics. Tests validate specs;
they are not a source of new requirements.

Semantic change path:

```text
AMEND SPECIFICATION → REVIEW → FREEZE → IMPLEMENT
```

Never silently change behavior to make tests pass.

## Gates

```text
GATE 0 — Product & Domain
GATE 1 — Economic Kernel
GATE 2 — Economic Invariants
GATE 3 — Simulator
GATE 4 — Adversarial Scenarios
GATE 5 — Regression
GATE 6 — Payment & Settlement
GATE 7 — x402
GATE 8 — Multi-Agent Economic World
```

Rules:

* Semantically close a gate before implementing it.
* Do not silently reopen a frozen gate.
* Later modules must not redefine earlier economic semantics.
* Specification alone does not authorize implementation.
* `READY_FOR_REVIEW` ≠ `FROZEN` ≠ `IMPLEMENTED` ≠ `PASS`.

Lifecycle:

```text
DRAFT → READY_FOR_REVIEW → REMEDIATION? → FROZEN
→ IMPLEMENTATION_AUTHORIZED → IMPLEMENTED → AUDITED → PASS
```

## Modules (do not blur)

```text
M01 Kernel | M02 Invariants | M03 Simulator | M04 Adversarial
M05 Chaos | M06 Regression | M07 Payment/Settlement | M08 x402 | M09 Multi-Agent
```

Move responsibilities only via explicit specification amendment.

## Missing requirements

Do not invent semantics. Identify `OD-XX` / `BLOCKER-XX` / `AMENDMENT-XX` and
stop if blocked.

## ADRs

Required for material decisions on determinism, numeric representation,
ordering, architecture, persistence, public API, security, integrations.
An ADR must not silently redefine a frozen specification.

## Agent role

Act as an engineering agent — not product owner, unauthorized architect, or
requirements inventor. If underspecified: **STOP → REPORT → identify gap**.
